## Evidence against

- **Holders capture little.** Over the last 365 days the Intents protocol kept {rev365} as revenue (about {bps} basis points of volume; all fees were {fbps} basis points). NEAR chain gas fees were {chain365}. NEAR's market cap is {mcap}: {mrev} yearly Intents revenue and {mchain} yearly chain fees. ![chart 4](charts/04_value_capture.png)
- **Levels point the wrong way for holders.** The correlation of log weekly Intents volume with log NEAR price is {lvl}: usage grew while the price fell. Usage alone has not supported the price.
- **The base chain shrank while Intents grew.** NearBlocks daily active accounts averaged {aa0} in the first 13 weeks of the window and {aa1} in the latest 13 weeks. Part of that drop may be bot or airdrop traffic leaving, and relayed signers are undercounted, but it is not the picture of a busier chain. Weekly changes correlate {aa} with price changes. ![chart 5](charts/05_active_accounts.png)
- **Stablecoins on NEAR shrank:** {st0} a year ago, {st1} now. A settlement layer for money should hold more dollars, not fewer. ![chart 6](charts/06_stablecoins.png)
- **The recent rally broke the pattern for a quarter.** Over the latest 13 weeks the NEAR price moved {pr13} and Intents volume {vr13} (4 week averages), and the weekly change correlation in those 13 weeks alone was {cl13}, above the 0.5 line. The 26 week test below still holds, but this is the first sign that usage and price may be moving together. Prediction P3 tests it.
- Volume counts can flatter. DefiLlama may count both legs or confidential flow; another entry in this track, [near-thesis](https://github.com/soldanoandrea/near-thesis), found public swaps match only part of DefiLlama's reported volume on sampled days. Our share numbers inherit that.

## Raw chain evidence: read from NEAR itself

Aggregators can be wrong, so the core supply and buyback facts are read straight from NEAR archival RPC: one block per week, the first block at or after Monday 00:00 UTC, {nblk} blocks from height {blk0} to {blk1} ([data/near_rpc.csv](data/near_rpc.csv)). Each row stores the block hash, so anyone can check it against any NEAR node. Code: `settlemark/chain.py`.

- **New supply.** Total NEAR supply is {sup1}. It grew by {iss52} ({iss52p}) in the last 52 weeks and {iss13} in the last 13. Issuance is the cost every holder pays.
- **Buybacks are real and measurable.** The wallet `buybacks.multisignature.near` first held NEAR in the week of {bbstart} and holds {bbnow} now. It gained {bb13} in the last 13 weeks, worth {bbusd13} at each week's price. Intents revenue over the same weeks was {rev13} by DefiLlama, so about {bbshare} of reported revenue turns into NEAR bought for holders. The two series line up closely, which suggests DefiLlama's revenue figure tracks this buyback flow. The wallet balance fell in {bbdown} sampled weeks, so no NEAR left it and the weekly gains are buybacks, not round trips.
- **But buybacks cover only {cov13} of new supply** over the last 13 weeks. Net supply still grows: holder capture is real but not yet enough to shrink the float. ![chart 7](charts/07_supply_vs_buybacks.png)
- **Usage inside the protocol grew on chain.** The `intents.near` contract stores {stor1} of state, up from {stor0} a year ago ({storx}). Weekly growth in that storage correlates {storp} with NEAR price changes and {storv} with Intents volume changes over {storw} weeks: a raw chain usage measure that does not follow price.
- **Cross checks.** Price times RPC supply gives a fully diluted value of {fdv} at {pxnow}; NearBlocks market cap is {mcfdv} of it, so the market cap used for F5 is consistent with the chain. On {bqd} days the BigQuery public dataset counts {bqr} receipt actions per NearBlocks transaction, so receipts are not a substitute for transactions in trend work.

