## Does Intents volume lead network usage?

The track asks whether cross chain volume through Intents is a better leading indicator of real network usage than the token price. Part 1 already shows price does not lead it. Here we test the leading part directly (`settlemark/leadlag.py`). Weekly log changes over {llw} weeks. For each usage series, a Granger style F test asks whether the last {llk} weeks of Intents changes improve a forecast of usage beyond usage's own past. A second, distribution free p value rotates the Intents series by every shift of at least 8 weeks, which keeps each series' own rhythm but breaks any real timing link. The lag order ({llk}) was fixed in `settlemark.toml` before we looked; orders 1 to 4 are in `results/results.json`.

{lltable}

- **Result: {llverdict} at p below {llalpha}.** The smallest headline p value is {llmin}. For NearBlocks active accounts the strongest lead is {llaak} week at {llaar}, inside the noise band, and the reverse test (usage leading Intents) gives {llaarev}. ![chart 10](charts/10_lead_lag.png)
- **Read the chart with the band in mind.** The band is plus or minus {llband}, the 95% range for one correlation when there is no link. Bars at positive leads that cross it: {llout} of {llbars} ({lloutwhat}). Chance alone would put about {llexp} there. The joint tests above, which weigh all lags at once, are the ones to trust.
- **Same week is different: {llsame}.** Intents settles on NEAR, so a busy Intents week mechanically adds NEAR transactions and `intents.near` state in that same week. That is co-movement, partly by construction, not a lead.

**Out of sample check.** The tests above use every week to fit and to judge. Here both forecasting models (usage's own {llk} lags, and the same plus {llk} lags of Intents) are fitted once on the first {lloostrain} weeks only, frozen, and then asked to forecast each of the last {lloostest} weeks, from the week of {lloossplit} on, one week ahead. The 70 week split was chosen on 15 Nov, after the in sample results above were known, so treat it as a robustness check, not a fresh test. Code: `out_of_sample` in `settlemark/stats.py`.

{oostable}

- **{lloosverdict}.** The best case is {lloosbest}; the smallest one sided Clark West p value is {lloosminp}, {lloosread}.
- {lloostest} test weeks is a small sample, so a modest real gain could still hide here. State size is also the series most tied to Intents by construction (Intents writes its own state).
- **So the track's hypothesis is not supported by these data.** Intents grew while base chain activity fell, and week to week the two move independently. That fits part 1 (Intents is its own flow), but it means Intents volume is not, on public data, an early signal of NEAR usage.
- Caveats: {lln} tests were run, so one p value under {llalpha} would be expected by chance; none came out that low. Two years of weekly data has low power, so a modest lead could exist and be missed. NearBlocks counts accounts, and relayers hide signers. A Granger test is about prediction, not cause. BigQuery weekly transactions are almost the same count as NearBlocks transactions (see the BigQuery section), so those two rows are one test twice, and the true number of independent tests is lower than {lln}.

## Against other cross chain routers

The same source for everyone (DefiLlama's free fees and volume APIs), the same {pedays} days ({pestart} to {peend}), {pen} routers. Code: `settlemark/peers.py`; data: `data/peers/`.

{petable}

- Intents is the largest of the {pen} by volume ({pevol}, {peshare} of the group). Its users pay {pefee} basis points in fees against a median of {pefeemed} for the other {peothers}; it is cheaper than {pecheap} of them. It keeps {perev} basis points as revenue against a peer median of {perevmed}. ![chart 11](charts/11_router_take_rates.png)
- **The holder gap is the sharpest number in this note.** NEAR's market cap is {pemrev} annualised Intents revenue; routers with their own token trade at a median {petok}. NEAR is priced {peprem} richer per dollar of Intents revenue. Part of that is fair, since NEAR is a whole layer 1 and not only Intents, but it is the size of the bet a holder makes on Intents growth. Prediction P12 tests whether the gap persists.
- Caveats: each number follows that protocol's DefiLlama methodology, and they differ. Intents fees are the protocol fee plus the affiliate fee each frontend charges; Intents revenue is NEAR swept into NEAR's revenue wallets (the same flow `revenue.near.org` and the buyback show). THORChain fees include liquidity provider fees, outbound fees and affiliate fees; LI.FI and Rango are aggregators that route over other bridges, possibly including NEAR Intents, so volumes can overlap. Only routers with daily volume and fees on the free API are in; Relay, Across and deBridge publish fees there, but their volume needs a paid key.

