## Verdict

Part 1 is supported over the full window and in every {rw} week window, but the latest quarter is the weakest evidence yet ({cl13}), and P3 will tell us within 8 weeks. Part 2 is supported and now measured on chain: about {bbshare} of Intents revenue already buys NEAR, yet buybacks cover only {cov13} of new supply, and a buyback valuation gives {vbase} a token in the base case against {pxnow}. The price assumes about {gimp} yearly growth in holder cash. On this model the price is justified only if Intents volume keeps compounding toward {bev} a week. This is research, not investment advice. Watch chart 7, not the price. The track's own hypothesis, that Intents volume leads network usage, is not supported: {llverdict},{llooswhere}. Against other routers, Intents is the biggest and mid priced, and per dollar of Intents revenue NEAR is priced {peprem} richer than router tokens are per dollar of their own revenue; part of that is fair, since NEAR is a whole layer 1, not only Intents.

## Limits

- The one command rebuild uses free public APIs only: DefiLlama, NearBlocks, CoinGecko and NEAR archival RPC (FastNEAR first, the NEAR Foundation node as fallback). The Intents Explorer API was not used (it needs a partner token). BigQuery results are committed and refreshed on demand (section above and `docs/BIGQUERY.md`).
- Correlations of weekly changes over about two years are noisy. The early Intents weeks are tiny, so log changes there are large.
- NearBlocks active accounts can miss signers hidden behind relayers.
- CoinGecko's free tier gives 365 days, so the Bitcoin test covers fewer weeks than the NEAR test.
- The valuation counts only buybacks. It ignores staking yield, which is paid in new supply, and any future fee switch. A {dr} discount rate is a choice; the table shows {dr} is neither the harshest nor the kindest.
- `python -m settlemark check` rebuilds everything from the committed CSVs and fails unless every output matches byte for byte and every number in this file is computed or is a constant written in the template.
- If a source changes its methodology, rerunning `npm run rebuild` changes the numbers. That is intended: nothing here is frozen by hand.
